**Narrowing is TypeScript refining a variable's type inside a branch, based on control flow: `typeof`, `instanceof`, `in`, equality checks, truthiness and custom type guards. A discriminated union gives each member a literal tag property, so checking the tag narrows to exactly one member, and assigning the leftover to `never` makes the compiler flag any case you forget.**

```ts
type RequestState =
  | { status: "idle" }
  | { status: "loading" }
  | { status: "success"; data: string[] }
  | { status: "error"; error: Error };

function assertNever(x: never): never {
  throw new Error(`Unhandled: ${JSON.stringify(x)}`);
}

function render(s: RequestState): string {
  switch (s.status) {
    case "idle": return "Start typing";
    case "loading": return "Loading...";
    case "success": return s.data.join(", ");   // data is available here
    case "error": return s.error.message;
    default: return assertNever(s);              // compile error if a case is missing
  }
}
```

Add `{ status: "cancelled" }` to the union and `assertNever(s)` fails to compile, pointing at every switch that needs updating.

**Why model state this way:** compared with `{ loading: boolean; data?: string[]; error?: Error }`, impossible combinations (loading and error at once, success without data) cannot be represented at all.

**Other narrowing tools**
- `typeof x === "string"`, `x instanceof Date`, `"swim" in animal`.
- Custom guards: `function isUser(x: unknown): x is User { ... }`.
- Assertion functions: `function assert(c: unknown): asserts c { ... }`.
- Since TS 5.5, `arr.filter((x) => x !== undefined)` infers the guard automatically.
- `satisfies never` is a lighter alternative to `assertNever` in the default branch.

**Gotchas**
- Narrowing does not survive across callbacks for `let` variables that could be reassigned; copy to a `const`.
- Destructuring the tag into a separate variable used to break narrowing; modern TS handles destructured discriminants when they are `const`.

**Generics are type parameters: they let a function, class or type work over many types while keeping the relationship between inputs and outputs. A constraint (`T extends X`) limits which types are allowed, so you can safely use members of `X` inside the generic code.**

```ts
// Without generics the return type is lost
function firstAny(arr: any[]): any { return arr[0]; }

// With generics the relationship is kept
function first<T>(arr: readonly T[]): T | undefined { return arr[0]; }
const n = first([1, 2, 3]);   // number | undefined

// Constraint: T must have an id
function byId<T extends { id: string }>(items: T[]): Map<string, T> {
  return new Map(items.map((item) => [item.id, item]));
}
byId([{ id: "a", name: "Ada" }]);   // Map<string, { id: string; name: string }>
byId([{ name: "x" }]);              // error: id is missing

// keyof constraint: K must be a key of T
function pluck<T, K extends keyof T>(items: T[], key: K): T[K][] {
  return items.map((item) => item[key]);
}
const names = pluck([{ id: "a", age: 3 }], "age");   // number[]
pluck([{ id: "a" }], "nope");                         // error
```

**Key ideas**
- **Inference:** callers rarely write `<T>`; TypeScript infers it from arguments.
- **Constraint vs annotation:** `byId(items: { id: string }[])` would return `Map<string, { id: string }>` and lose the `name` field. The generic preserves the caller's exact type.
- **Defaults:** `type ApiResponse<T = unknown> = { data: T }`.
- **`const` type parameters** (TS 5.0): `function f<const T>(x: T)` infers literal types like `readonly ["a", "b"]` instead of `string[]`.

**Gotchas**
- A type parameter used only once is usually pointless; `function log<T>(x: T): void` is just `(x: unknown) => void`.
- Do not return `T` from something that cannot produce it, such as `function parse<T>(json: string): T`, which is an unchecked cast in disguise. Validate instead.

**Utility types are built-in generic types that transform other types. The most used are `Partial`, `Required`, `Readonly`, `Pick`, `Omit`, `Record`, `ReturnType`, `Parameters`, `Awaited`, `NonNullable`, `Exclude` and `Extract`. Most are a few lines of mapped or conditional types.**

```ts
interface User { id: string; name: string; email: string; age?: number }

type UserPatch = Partial<Omit<User, "id">>;        // all optional, no id
type UserPreview = Pick<User, "id" | "name">;
type ByRole = Record<"admin" | "member", User[]>;
type Fetched = Awaited<ReturnType<typeof fetchUser>>;
type Args = Parameters<typeof fetchUser>;           // tuple of params
```

**Implementing them with mapped types**

```ts
type MyPartial<T> = { [K in keyof T]?: T[K] };
type MyReadonly<T> = { readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K] };
type MyRequired<T> = { [K in keyof T]-?: T[K] };    // -? removes optional
type MyPick<T, K extends keyof T> = { [P in K]: T[P] };
type MyRecord<K extends PropertyKey, V> = { [P in K]: V };
type MyOmit<T, K extends PropertyKey> = Pick<T, Exclude<keyof T, K>>;

// Key remapping with "as" (TS 4.1+)
type Getters<T> = {
  [K in keyof T as `get${Capitalize<string & K>}`]: () => T[K];
};
type UserGetters = Getters<Pick<User, "name">>;     // { getName: () => string }
```

**Mechanism:** `keyof T` produces a union of keys, `[K in ...]` iterates over it, `T[K]` looks up each property type. Modifiers `?` and `readonly` can be added with `+` or removed with `-`.

**Gotchas**
- `Partial` and `Readonly` are shallow; nested objects keep their original types. Write a recursive `DeepPartial` if needed.
- `Omit` is not strict: `Omit<User, "typo">` compiles because `K` is any `PropertyKey`. Some teams define a `StrictOmit<T, K extends keyof T>`.
- `Omit` on a union type does not distribute; it collapses the union to common keys. Use a distributive version (`T extends unknown ? Omit<T, K> : never`).

**Most modern TypeScript codebases prefer union literal types (often derived from an `as const` object) over `enum`. Unions are erased at compile time, work with plain strings from JSON, and play well with narrowing; enums generate runtime code and have some quirks, especially numeric ones.**

```ts
// Enum: emits a runtime object
enum Status { Active = "active", Archived = "archived" }
function setA(s: Status) {}
setA(Status.Active);
setA("active");               // error: string literal is not the enum

// Union literal: zero runtime cost
type Status2 = "active" | "archived";
function setB(s: Status2) {}
setB("active");               // ok, data from an API just works

// Need runtime values too? Derive the type from a const object
const STATUS = { Active: "active", Archived: "archived" } as const;
type Status3 = (typeof STATUS)[keyof typeof STATUS];   // "active" | "archived"
const all = Object.values(STATUS);                     // iterate at runtime
```

**Enum quirks**
- **Numeric enums are unsafe:** before TS 5.0 any number was assignable to a numeric enum; they also create reverse mappings (`Dir[0] === "Up"`), so `Object.values` returns both names and numbers.
- **Nominal-ish:** string enums reject matching string literals, which is awkward for API data.
- **Not just types:** they emit JavaScript, and they are not supported by "type-stripping only" setups such as Node's built-in TypeScript support or `erasableSyntaxOnly`.
- **`const enum`** is inlined but breaks with isolated module transpilers (esbuild, SWC, Babel) across files.

**When enums are still fine:** existing codebases that use them consistently, or when you want a named namespace for values. Either way, be consistent.

**With zod:** `z.enum(["active", "archived"])` gives runtime validation and `z.infer` produces the union, which is the usual pattern at API boundaries.

**Declaration merging is TypeScript combining multiple declarations with the same name into one definition, for example two `interface` blocks, or a namespace merged with a function. Module augmentation uses that to add types to a third-party module or the global scope without editing its source, such as adding a `user` field to Express's `Request` or a custom variable to `Window`.**

**What merges**
- Interface with interface: members combine (same-named methods become overloads).
- Namespace with class, function or enum: adds static members.
- Type aliases never merge; classes do not merge with classes.

```ts
// types/express.d.ts: augment a module
import "express";
declare module "express-serve-static-core" {
  interface Request {
    user?: { id: string; role: "admin" | "member" };
  }
}

// types/global.d.ts: augment the global scope
export {};
declare global {
  interface Window { __APP_VERSION__: string }
}

// Augmenting a library's config interface
declare module "@tanstack/react-query" {
  interface Register { defaultError: AppError }
}
```

**Rules that trip people up**
- The augmentation file must be a module (it needs an `import` or `export`), otherwise `declare module "x"` creates a brand new ambient module that replaces the real types.
- You can only add declarations, not change the type of an existing property.
- The file must be included by `tsconfig.json` (`include` or `types`).
- Augment the module that actually declares the interface (for Express that is `express-serve-static-core`).

**Libraries design for this:** many expose an empty interface (`Register`, `Theme`, `CustomTypeOptions`) purely so apps can augment it once and get typed results everywhere. That is how i18n keys, router paths and styled-components themes become type-safe.

**Trade-off:** augmentation is global and invisible at the use site; a `req.user` typed as always present is a lie if the auth middleware did not run. Keep augmented fields optional, or narrow them in the handler.

**Types are erased when compiled, so TypeScript cannot check data that arrives at runtime (API responses, form posts, `localStorage`, env vars). The safe pattern is to treat that data as `unknown`, validate it with a schema library like zod at the boundary, and derive the static type from the schema so the two can never drift apart.**

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

const UserSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string().uuid(),
  name: z.string().min(1),
  role: z.enum(["admin", "member"]),
  createdAt: z.coerce.date(),
});
type User = z.infer<typeof UserSchema>;

async function getUser(id: string): Promise<User> {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
  const json: unknown = await res.json();
  return UserSchema.parse(json);   // throws with details if the shape is wrong
}
```

**Why not just cast?** `(await res.json()) as User` compiles, but if the API sends `role: "owner"` or omits `name`, the bug surfaces far away as `undefined is not a function`. Validation fails fast at the edge with a clear message.

**Where to validate**
- **Server inputs:** every route handler and server action body, query string and params (never trust the client).
- **External APIs and LLM output:** shapes change without notice.
- **Environment variables** at startup, so a missing secret fails the deploy instead of a request.
- **Forms:** share one schema between client (instant feedback) and server (the real check).

**Typing components safely**

```ts
type ButtonProps = React.ComponentProps<"button"> & { variant?: "primary" | "ghost" };
function Button({ variant = "primary", ...rest }: ButtonProps) { /* ... */ }
```

Props are compile-time only, which is fine because they come from your own code; validate where untrusted data enters, then pass typed values down.

**Trade-offs:** validation has a runtime and bundle cost, so validate once at the boundary, not in every function. Use `safeParse` when you want to handle errors without exceptions, and `.transform` to normalise data (dates, trimming) in the same step.

**A sound type system guarantees that a value's runtime type always matches its static type. TypeScript explicitly does not aim for that; its design goals trade soundness for compatibility with existing JavaScript patterns and productivity. The main holes are `any` and assertions, unchecked index access, covariant mutable arrays, bivariant method parameters, user-written type predicates, and narrowing that is not invalidated by mutation.**

```ts
// 1. any and assertions
const user = JSON.parse(body) as User;     // no check at all
const el = document.querySelector("#x")!;  // non-null assertion

// 2. Index access (without noUncheckedIndexedAccess)
const xs: number[] = [];
const n: number = xs[0];                   // actually undefined
const byId: Record<string, User> = {};
byId["missing"].name;                      // TypeError at runtime

// 3. Array covariance
const dogs: Dog[] = [];
const animals: Animal[] = dogs;
animals.push(new Cat());                   // dogs now holds a Cat

// 4. Method parameter bivariance
interface Listener { handle(e: Event): void }
const l: Listener = { handle(e: MouseEvent) { e.clientX; } };  // compiles

// 5. Lying predicates
const isUser = (x: unknown): x is User => true;

// 6. Narrowing survives mutation
function f(o: { v: string | null }) {
  if (o.v !== null) { reset(o); o.v.length; }   // reset may set v = null
}
```

**Why these exist.**
- Arrays: `Array<T>` declares `push` as a method; checking it strictly would make `Dog[]` not assignable to `Animal[]`, breaking huge amounts of code. Method bivariance is the general version of that decision.
- Index access: making every `arr[i]` possibly undefined is noisy, so it is opt-in.
- Narrowing: resetting every property narrowing after each function call would make narrowing almost useless.
- `any` and `as`: escape hatches for gradual adoption of an untyped ecosystem.

**Mitigations a senior engineer actually uses.**
- `strict`, plus `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` and `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`.
- Callback members declared as properties (`handle: (e: Event) => void`), which `strictFunctionTypes` checks contravariantly.
- `readonly T[]` / `ReadonlyArray<T>` in parameters, which removes the unsafe write path and documents intent.
- Parse untrusted data with a schema at boundaries instead of `as`; ban return-only generics like `fetchJson<T>()`.
- Lint rules: `@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any`, `no-unsafe-assignment`, `no-unsafe-member-access`, `no-non-null-assertion`; `@ts-expect-error` instead of `@ts-ignore`.
- Copy narrowed values into `const` before calling code that might mutate them.

**Framing for the interviewer:** the goal is not a sound system but a predictable one. Know where the holes are, close the cheap ones with flags and lint, and put runtime validation at every place where the types are a claim rather than a guarantee.

**A function should be generic when a type parameter relates two or more positions: an input to the output, or one input to another. If T appears only once, it relates nothing and should be replaced by its constraint (often `unknown`). This is the "golden rule of generics" from Dan Vanderkam's Effective TypeScript.**

**Generics that add nothing.**

```ts
function logIt<T>(x: T): void { console.log(x); }       // same as (x: unknown): void
function len<T extends { length: number }>(x: T): number { return x.length; }
// same as (x: { length: number }): number
```

Both compile and both mislead the reader into looking for a relationship that is not there.

**The dangerous one: return-only generics.**

```ts
async function fetchJson<T>(url: string): Promise<T> {
  return (await fetch(url)).json();
}
const user = await fetchJson<User>("/api/me");   // looks typed, checks nothing
```

T appears only in the output, so the caller chooses it and nothing verifies it. It is an `as` cast with nicer syntax. Better: return `unknown`, or take a schema so T is inferred from something that checks:

```ts
async function fetchJson<T>(url: string, schema: z.ZodType<T>): Promise<T> {
  return schema.parse(await (await fetch(url)).json());
}
const user = await fetchJson("/api/me", UserSchema);   // T inferred and validated
```

**Generics that earn their keep.**

```ts
// Output relates to input
function first<T>(xs: readonly T[]): T | undefined { return xs[0]; }

// Two inputs relate
function get<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] { return obj[key]; }
get(user, "email");   // string; get(user, "emial") is an error

// Preserving the caller's precise type
function withId<T extends object>(o: T): T & { id: string } {
  return { ...o, id: crypto.randomUUID() };
}
withId({ name: "a", tags: ["x"] });   // { name: string; tags: string[] } & { id: string }
```

The last example shows why you return `T` (or something built from it) rather than the constraint: returning `object` would throw away everything the caller knows.

**Alternatives to a generic.**
- **Overloads** for a small, closed set of input-output pairs where a conditional return type would be unreadable: `parse(x: string): number; parse(x: string[]): number[]`.
- **Union parameters** when the output does not depend on which member came in.
- **Separate functions** when a single generic needs a long conditional type to describe what it returns.

**Signal in code review:** if callers routinely write explicit type arguments (`foo<User>()`), the signature is probably not inferring from anything real, which usually means a return-only generic or a missing relationship.

**TypeScript's control-flow analysis narrows a union when it sees ordinary JavaScript checks: `typeof`, `instanceof`, the `in` operator, equality comparisons (`===`, `!==`, `== null`), truthiness, `Array.isArray`, and calls to user-defined type predicates. Each has a gotcha: `typeof null` is "object", truthiness drops 0 and "", `instanceof` only works for classes in the same realm, and `in` only proves a key exists, not what its value is.**

```ts
function describe(x: string | number | Date | null | { id: string }) {
  if (x === null) return "null";                 // equality
  if (typeof x === "string") return x.trim();    // typeof
  if (typeof x === "number") return x.toFixed(); // typeof
  if (x instanceof Date) return x.toISOString(); // instanceof
  if ("id" in x) return x.id;                    // in
  return x;                                       // never: all cases handled
}
```

**The gotchas.**

- **`typeof null === "object"`.** TypeScript models it, so `typeof x === "object"` on `string | object | null` leaves `object | null`. Check `x !== null` too.
- **Truthiness.** `if (value)` removes `null` and `undefined` but also `0`, `""`, `NaN` and `false`:

```ts
function label(count: number | undefined) {
  if (!count) return "No items";     // 0 items and "unknown" are now the same
  return `${count} items`;
}
// Better: if (count === undefined) ...
```

- **`== null`** is the one useful loose equality: it matches both `null` and `undefined`.
- **`instanceof`** checks the prototype chain at runtime. It cannot work with interfaces or type aliases (they do not exist at runtime), and it fails across realms: an array or error from an iframe, a worker, or a different copy of a library is not an `instanceof` your class. Prefer `Array.isArray`, structural checks or discriminant fields.
- **`in`** narrows to union members that declare the key. Since TypeScript 4.9 it also narrows `unknown`-ish objects to `T & Record<"key", unknown>`, which is handy when parsing. But a property can exist with value `undefined`, and `in` also sees inherited properties.
- **`Array.isArray`** on `unknown` gives `any[]`, so elements are unchecked.
- **Equality between two variables** narrows both to their common type, which is occasionally surprising: `if (a === b)` with `a: string | number` and `b: string | boolean` makes both `string`.

**Narrowing `unknown` safely.**

```ts
function getId(v: unknown): string | undefined {
  if (typeof v === "object" && v !== null && "id" in v && typeof v.id === "string") {
    return v.id;
  }
}
```

This chain works without casts since 4.9. For anything bigger than a couple of fields, a schema parse (Zod, Valibot) is clearer and gives error messages.

**Discriminants beat structural checks.** When you control the type, add a literal `kind` or `type` field and switch on it. It is faster to read, survives refactors, and enables exhaustiveness checks with `never`.

**A type predicate is a function returning boolean whose return type `x is T` tells the compiler to narrow x to T in the true branch (and exclude T in the false branch). An assertion function returns nothing and throws if the check fails; its return type `asserts x is T` (or `asserts condition`) narrows x for the rest of the scope after the call. Both are trusted claims: the compiler does not verify that the body actually checks what the signature says.**

```ts
interface User { id: string; email: string }

function isUser(v: unknown): v is User {
  return typeof v === "object" && v !== null
    && "id" in v && typeof v.id === "string"
    && "email" in v && typeof v.email === "string";
}

function assertIsUser(v: unknown): asserts v is User {
  if (!isUser(v)) throw new TypeError("Expected a User");
}

// Predicate: you branch
if (isUser(data)) send(data.email);
else showError();

// Assertion: the rest of the function assumes success
const body: unknown = await req.json();
assertIsUser(body);
body.email;   // User
```

**Choosing.**

```text
x is T            caller decides what to do on failure; both paths continue
asserts x is T    failure is exceptional; code after the call is the happy path
asserts cond      generic invariant: assert(user !== null), assert(items.length > 0)
```

Predicates fit filtering and UI branching (`items.filter(isVisible)`). Assertions fit invariants and tests, where failing should stop execution.

**Inferred predicates (TypeScript 5.5).** The compiler now infers `x is T` for simple arrow functions, which fixes the long-standing filter annoyance:

```ts
const ids = [1, undefined, 3].filter((x) => x !== undefined);   // number[]
const bad = [1, 0, undefined].filter((x) => !!x);                // (number | undefined)[]
```

The second is not inferred because `!!x` returns false for `0`, which is a number; a predicate must be truthful in both directions.

**The explicit-annotation rule.** Assertion calls only narrow when every name in the call target has a declared type. A `function` declaration is fine; `const assertX = (v: unknown): asserts v is X => {...}` is not (TS2775), but `const assertX: (v: unknown) => asserts v is X = ...` is. The reason is that control-flow analysis needs to know the function is an assertion without inferring types mid-analysis.

**Keeping them honest.**
- Predicates lie easily: `(v): v is User => true` compiles. Keep them small, unit-test both outcomes, or generate them from a schema (`UserSchema.safeParse(v).success` wrapped in a predicate).
- The false branch matters: if a predicate returns false for some valid T values, the else branch is narrowed wrongly.
- For untrusted data, prefer parsing (which returns a new typed value and error details) over predicates on the original value.

**`Record<K, V>` and an index signature (`{ [key: string]: V }`) are two spellings of a type for a plain object used as a dictionary; `Record` with a finite union of keys is the special, very useful case that forces every key to exist. `Map<K, V>` is a different runtime data structure. Use a finite-key Record for lookup tables, a Record or index signature for JSON-shaped dictionaries, and a Map when keys are not strings, when you add and delete a lot, or when you need insertion order and size.**

**Finite keys: exhaustive lookup tables.**

```ts
type Status = "draft" | "review" | "live";

const statusLabel: Record<Status, string> = {
  draft: "Draft",
  review: "In review",
  live: "Published",
};
```

Add `"archived"` to `Status` and this object fails to compile until you add a label. That is a cheap exhaustiveness check for data rather than code.

**Open keys: dictionaries.**

```ts
type UsersById = Record<string, User>;
type UsersById2 = { [id: string]: User };    // equivalent

const users: UsersById = {};
users["u1"].name;    // compiles; crashes if u1 is missing
```

By default, reading a key from a string-keyed record is typed as `User`, not `User | undefined`. Turn on `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` and it becomes `User | undefined`, forcing a check. Many teams consider this flag essential precisely because of dictionaries and arrays.

Index signatures can do a few things `Record` cannot spell as neatly: mix with known properties (`{ id: string; [k: string]: string }`, where known properties must match the index type), use template literal patterns (`` { [k: `data-${string}`]: string } ``), and carry parameter names that document intent (`[userId: string]`).

**Map: a runtime decision.**

```ts
const cache = new Map<string, User>();
cache.set(id, user);
const hit = cache.get(id);   // User | undefined, honestly typed
```

Choose Map when:
- keys are objects or numbers you do not want coerced to strings;
- the collection churns (frequent `delete`; objects can degrade in engines when used as hash maps with many deletes);
- you need `size`, guaranteed insertion-order iteration or easy iteration of entries;
- keys might collide with `Object.prototype` names like `__proto__` or `constructor` (or use `Object.create(null)`).

Choose a plain object when the data is serialised to JSON (Maps serialise to `{}` unless you convert them), when it is small and static, or when a library expects plain objects (React props, Redux state, query params).

**Related.** `Partial<Record<Status, string>>` is a table where some keys may be missing. `Object.keys(record)` returns `string[]`, not `Status[]`, because structural typing allows extra keys; use a typed helper or iterate a known key array (`STATUSES.map(s => statusLabel[s])`).

**Pick one source of truth (a function, a constant, a schema) and compute the other types from it. `typeof` turns a value into its type, `keyof` gives a union of an object type's keys, indexed access (`T["key"]`, `T[number]`) pulls out a member type, and `ReturnType`, `Parameters` and `Awaited` read a function's signature. When the source changes, every derived type follows and the compiler points at the code that no longer fits.**

```text
value  --typeof-->  type  --keyof-->  key union
type   --T[K]-->    property type     --T[number]-->  array element type
fn     --typeof-->  fn type  --ReturnType / Parameters-->  pieces   --Awaited-->  unwrapped
```

**From a function.**

```ts
export async function searchProducts(params: { q: string; page?: number; sort?: "price" | "rating" }) {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/products?${toQuery(params)}`);
  return ProductPageSchema.parse(await res.json());
}

type SearchParams = Parameters<typeof searchProducts>[0];
type SearchResult = Awaited<ReturnType<typeof searchProducts>>;
type Product = SearchResult["items"][number];
type SortKey = NonNullable<SearchParams["sort"]>;   // "price" | "rating"
```

Note the steps: `typeof` is required because `searchProducts` is a value; `ReturnType` gives `Promise<ProductPage>`; `Awaited` unwraps it (recursively, including nested promises and thenables).

**From constants.**

```ts
export const THEMES = { light: { bg: "#fff" }, dark: { bg: "#000" } } as const;
export type ThemeName = keyof typeof THEMES;       // "light" | "dark"

export const PLANS = ["free", "pro", "team"] as const;
export type Plan = (typeof PLANS)[number];         // "free" | "pro" | "team"
```

`keyof typeof THEMES` reads as "keys of the type of the value THEMES"; `keyof THEMES` alone is an error because THEMES is not a type.

**From a schema** (usually the best source when data crosses a boundary):

```ts
const OrderSchema = z.object({ id: z.string(), total: z.number(), status: z.enum(["paid", "refunded"]) });
type Order = z.infer<typeof OrderSchema>;
type OrderStatus = Order["status"];
```

**From libraries.** `React.ComponentProps<typeof Button>` reads another component's props; `Parameters<typeof router.push>[0]` gives a router's accepted href type. This avoids importing internal types that a library does not export.

**Trade-offs.**
- Derived types can be unreadable in hovers (`Awaited<ReturnType<typeof x>>["items"][number]`). Give them names, and use a `Prettify` helper to flatten intersections.
- Deriving from implementation couples types to implementation details. For a public module boundary, a named exported type (checked against the implementation with `satisfies` or an explicit return type) can be a more stable contract.
- `ReturnType` on an overloaded function uses the last overload, which may not be the one you want.
- Annotating exported functions' return types explicitly is still good practice in libraries: it keeps declaration files small, speeds up checking, and prevents accidental API changes when the body changes.

**Omit and Pick are mapped types over `keyof T`, and `keyof` of a union only contains the keys common to every member. So `Omit<A | B, "x">` collapses the union into one object with only the shared properties, losing member-specific fields and the discriminant's link to them. A distributive Omit wraps the operation in a conditional type on a naked type parameter, which applies Omit to each member separately: `type DistributiveOmit<T, K extends PropertyKey> = T extends unknown ? Omit<T, K> : never`.**

```ts
type Field =
  | { kind: "text"; id: string; maxLength: number }
  | { kind: "select"; id: string; options: string[] };

type NewField = Omit<Field, "id">;
// { kind: "text" | "select" }        maxLength and options are gone

type DistributiveOmit<T, K extends PropertyKey> = T extends unknown ? Omit<T, K> : never;
type NewField2 = DistributiveOmit<Field, "id">;
// { kind: "text"; maxLength: number } | { kind: "select"; options: string[] }
```

**Why it happens.** `Omit<T, K>` is `Pick<T, Exclude<keyof T, K>>`. For a union, `keyof (A | B)` is `keyof A & keyof B` in effect: a value of type `A | B` is only guaranteed to have the keys both share. Pick then builds a single object type over those keys, with each property's type being the union across members. The result is a valid but much less useful type.

**Why the fix works.** A conditional type whose checked type is a naked type parameter distributes over unions: `DistributiveOmit<A | B, K>` becomes `Omit<A, K> | Omit<B, K>`. `T extends unknown` is always true, so the condition exists purely to trigger distribution. The same trick fixes `Pick`, `Partial` with key lists, and any helper built on `keyof`.

**Key checking.** The built-in is declared `Omit<T, K extends keyof any>`, so `Omit<User, "emial">` compiles and removes nothing. It was intentionally left loose (so you can omit keys that only some types have), but in application code a strict version catches typos:

```ts
type StrictOmit<T, K extends keyof T> = Omit<T, K>;
```

For unions, combine both: `type StrictDistributiveOmit<T, K extends keyof T> = T extends unknown ? Omit<T, K> : never` (here `keyof T` is the common keys, which is usually what you want to omit).

**Where it bites: React props.**

```tsx
type ButtonProps =
  | ({ href: string } & React.ComponentProps<"a">)
  | ({ href?: never } & React.ComponentProps<"button">);

type IconButtonProps = Omit<ButtonProps, "children"> & { icon: Icon };   // union collapsed
type IconButtonProps2 = DistributiveOmit<ButtonProps, "children"> & { icon: Icon };   // correct
```

With the plain Omit, the wrapper loses the link between `href` and anchor-only attributes, and narrowing on `href` inside the component no longer works. Design-system libraries commonly ship a DistributiveOmit helper for exactly this reason.

**Cost.** Distribution runs once per member; for unions of hundreds of members (big generated prop unions) this adds noticeable check time, so measure if a wrapper type feels slow in the editor.

**A mapped type iterates over a union of keys and produces a property per key: `{ [K in Keys]: ... }`. The `as` clause (TypeScript 4.1) remaps each key to a new key, which can be a template literal type built from the old one, or `never` to drop it. Template literal types build string literal types from other types and distribute over unions, and the intrinsic helpers `Capitalize`, `Uppercase` and friends transform them.**

**Event handler names.**

```ts
type Events = {
  click: { x: number; y: number };
  focus: undefined;
  change: { value: string };
};

type Handlers<E> = {
  [K in keyof E & string as `on${Capitalize<K>}`]: (payload: E[K]) => void;
};

type H = Handlers<Events>;
// {
//   onClick: (payload: { x: number; y: number }) => void;
//   onFocus: (payload: undefined) => void;
//   onChange: (payload: { value: string }) => void;
// }
```

Pieces:
- `keyof E & string` (or `string & K` inside the template) because `keyof` may include `number` and `symbol`, which template literals do not accept.
- `` `on${Capitalize<K>}` `` creates the new name.
- `E[K]` still refers to the original key, so each handler keeps its own payload type. A ``Record<`on${...}`, Handler>`` could not do that.

**Filtering keys.** Map unwanted keys to `never`:

```ts
type FunctionKeys<T> = { [K in keyof T as T[K] extends (...args: any[]) => any ? K : never]: T[K] };
type DataOnly<T> = { [K in keyof T as T[K] extends Function ? never : K]: T[K] };
```

**Reverse direction: parsing names.** Template literals with `infer` can strip prefixes:

```ts
type EventFromHandler<H> = H extends `on${infer E}` ? Uncapitalize<E> : never;
type E1 = EventFromHandler<"onClick">;   // "click"
```

**Distribution in template slots.**

```ts
type Breakpoint = "sm" | "md" | "lg";
type Prop = "p" | "m";
type Utility = `${Breakpoint}:${Prop}-${1 | 2 | 4}`;   // 3 * 2 * 3 = 18 members
```

The cross product grows multiplicatively; the compiler refuses unions that become too large ("Expression produces a union type that is too complex to represent").

**Homomorphic mapped types.** A mapped type of the form `{ [K in keyof T]: ... }` is homomorphic: it copies `?` and `readonly` from T and maps arrays and tuples element-wise. That is why `Partial<T>` keeps existing optional properties optional and `Readonly<[number, string]>` is a readonly tuple. Remapping with `as` over plain `keyof T` still copies the modifiers, so a renamed optional property stays optional. A subtle trap: writing `[K in keyof T & string as ...]` is no longer homomorphic and drops `?`, so when modifiers matter, iterate `keyof T` and put `string & K` inside the template instead.

**Where this is used in real code.** Typed event emitters, design-system prop generation (`size`, `smSize`, `mdSize`), CSS-in-TS utility names, i18n key paths, and form libraries that compute `"address.city"` paths. In each case it removes a whole category of string typos.

**Judgement.** These types are cheap at the scale above. Trouble starts with recursive remapping over deep objects, which is where you should measure with `--extendedDiagnostics` and bound recursion depth.

**Type props as a plain object type on a plain function component, use `React.ReactNode` for children, and extend `React.ComponentProps<"button">` (or another element or component) to accept every native attribute, spreading the rest onto the element. When props depend on each other, use a union so invalid combinations do not compile.**

```tsx
type CardProps = {
  title: string;
  footer?: React.ReactNode;
  children: React.ReactNode;
};

export function Card({ title, footer, children }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <article>
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      <div>{children}</div>
      {footer}
    </article>
  );
}
```

**Children.** `React.ReactNode` is everything React can render: elements, strings, numbers, arrays, fragments, portals, `null`, `undefined`, booleans. `JSX.Element` (or `React.ReactElement`) is a single element, so `<Card>Hello</Card>` would fail. Use `ReactElement` only when you really need exactly one element, for example a component that clones its child.

**DOM passthrough.**

```tsx
type ButtonProps = React.ComponentProps<"button"> & {
  variant?: "primary" | "secondary";
  loading?: boolean;
};

export function Button({ variant = "primary", loading = false, disabled, children, ...rest }: ButtonProps) {
  return (
    <button {...rest} disabled={disabled || loading} data-variant={variant}>
      {loading ? <Spinner /> : children}
    </button>
  );
}
```

Callers get every native prop (`type`, `onClick`, `aria-*`, `form`) with correct types, and in React 19 `ref` too. Use `React.ComponentPropsWithoutRef<"button">` if you do not want to accept a ref, `React.ComponentProps<typeof Other>` to borrow another component's props, and `Omit<React.ComponentProps<"input">, "size">` when your prop replaces a native one with a different type.

**React.FC.** Since the React 18 types, `React.FC` no longer adds an implicit `children` prop, so it is not wrong. Plain functions with typed props are still the common choice: they read naturally, support generic components, and are what react.dev uses.

**Dependent props with unions.**

```tsx
type ModalProps =
  | { dismissible: true; onClose: () => void }
  | { dismissible?: false; onClose?: never };

type LinkButtonProps =
  | ({ href: string } & React.ComponentProps<"a">)
  | ({ href?: never } & React.ComponentProps<"button">);
```

`?: never` says "this prop must be absent in this variant", which lets TypeScript narrow inside the component and reject `<Modal dismissible />` without `onClose`.

**Defaults.** Use destructuring defaults (`variant = "primary"`) rather than `defaultProps`, which is not supported for function components in React 19.

**Server Components.** Props are typed the same way, but values passed from a Server Component to a Client Component must be serialisable (no functions except Server Actions, no class instances). The type system will not catch that for you; the framework reports it at runtime or build time.

**Let inline handlers infer their event type from the JSX attribute, and use React's event types (`React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>`, `React.FormEvent<HTMLFormElement>`) when the handler is defined separately. Give `useState` an explicit type when the initial value is `null` or an empty array, model multi-state logic with a reducer over discriminated unions, and type DOM refs with `useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null)`, which in React 19 returns `RefObject<HTMLInputElement | null>`.**

**Events.**

```tsx
function SignupForm() {
  const [email, setEmail] = useState("");

  const onChange = (e: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => setEmail(e.target.value);
  const onSubmit = (e: React.FormEvent<HTMLFormElement>) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    const data = new FormData(e.currentTarget);
  };

  return (
    <form onSubmit={onSubmit}>
      <input type="email" value={email} onChange={onChange} />
      <button onClick={(e) => e.shiftKey && track("shift-submit")}>Sign up</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

The inline `onClick` gets `React.MouseEvent<HTMLButtonElement>` for free. `e.currentTarget` is typed as the element the handler is attached to; `e.target` is whatever element dispatched the event (possibly a child), so for most events it is just `EventTarget`. Change events are the exception, where `target` is the input itself. When unsure, hover the attribute; there are also handler aliases such as `React.ChangeEventHandler<HTMLInputElement>`.

**State.**

```tsx
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);                 // inferred number
const [user, setUser] = useState<User | null>(null);   // explicit, null alone infers null
const [tags, setTags] = useState<string[]>([]);        // explicit, [] infers never[]
```

**Reducers with discriminated unions.**

```tsx
type State =
  | { status: "idle" }
  | { status: "saving" }
  | { status: "error"; message: string };
type Action = { type: "save" } | { type: "fail"; message: string } | { type: "reset" };

function reducer(state: State, action: Action): State {
  switch (action.type) {
    case "save":  return { status: "saving" };
    case "fail":  return { status: "error", message: action.message };
    case "reset": return { status: "idle" };
  }
}
const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, { status: "idle" });
```

`dispatch({ type: "fail" })` without a message is a compile error, and components that read `state.message` must first check `state.status === "error"`.

**Refs.**

```tsx
const inputRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);   // DOM ref: RefObject<HTMLInputElement | null>
const intervalId = useRef<number | null>(null);     // mutable box for non-render data

useEffect(() => inputRef.current?.focus(), []);
```

React 19's types require an argument to `useRef` and return a mutable `RefObject` in all cases, removing the old `MutableRefObject` vs `RefObject` confusion. Function components also receive `ref` as a normal prop now:

```tsx
function TextField({ ref, ...props }: React.ComponentProps<"input">) {
  return <input ref={ref} {...props} />;
}
```

So `forwardRef` is not needed in new code, and generic components no longer lose their type parameters to it.

**Context.** Default to `null` and expose a hook that throws, so consumers get a non-null type:

```tsx
const CartContext = createContext<Cart | null>(null);
export function useCart() {
  const cart = useContext(CartContext);
  if (!cart) throw new Error("useCart must be used within CartProvider");
  return cart;
}
```

**Custom hooks** returning tuples need `as const` (`return [value, setValue] as const`), otherwise the result is an array of a union and destructuring loses the types.

**A generic component is just a generic function: declare a type parameter, use it in the props type, and JSX infers it from the props you pass, exactly like arguments to a call. That lets data flow from `options` or `rows` into every callback (`onChange`, `renderCell`, `getKey`) with no casts.**

**A typed Select.**

```tsx
type SelectProps<T> = {
  options: readonly T[];
  value: T | null;
  onChange: (value: T) => void;
  getLabel: (option: T) => string;
  getKey: (option: T) => string;
};

export function Select<T>({ options, value, onChange, getLabel, getKey }: SelectProps<T>) {
  return (
    <ul role="listbox">
      {options.map((o) => (
        <li
          key={getKey(o)}
          role="option"
          aria-selected={value !== null && getKey(value) === getKey(o)}
          onClick={() => onChange(o)}
        >
          {getLabel(o)}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}

<Select
  options={countries}               // Country[]  ->  T = Country
  value={country}
  getKey={(c) => c.code}            // c: Country
  getLabel={(c) => c.name}
  onChange={setCountry}             // must accept Country
/>;
```

If you pass a `setUser` to `onChange` here, it fails to compile. You can also pin T explicitly: `<Select<Country> ... />`.

**A typed Table with checked column keys.**

```tsx
type Column<Row> = {
  [K in keyof Row & string]: {
    key: K;
    header: string;
    render?: (value: Row[K], row: Row) => React.ReactNode;
  };
}[keyof Row & string];

type TableProps<Row> = {
  rows: readonly Row[];
  columns: readonly Column<Row>[];
  rowKey: (row: Row) => string;
};

export function Table<Row>({ rows, columns, rowKey }: TableProps<Row>) {
  return (
    <table>
      <thead><tr>{columns.map((c) => <th key={c.key}>{c.header}</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map((row) => (
          <tr key={rowKey(row)}>
            {columns.map((c) => (
              <td key={c.key}>{c.render ? c.render(row[c.key] as never, row) : String(row[c.key])}</td>
            ))}
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  );
}
```

`Column<Row>` is a union with one member per key, so `{ key: "price", render: (v) => v.toFixed(2) }` types `v` as `number`, and `{ key: "prise" }` is an error. Inside the component the union is too wide to call `render` without a small cast; that trade (precise API, one internal cast) is normal in library code.

**Syntax details.**
- In `.tsx`, `const List = <T,>(props: ListProps<T>) => ...` needs the trailing comma (or `<T extends unknown>`), because `<T>` parses as a JSX tag.
- Constrain when you need members: `<T extends { id: string }>` lets you default `getKey` to `(o) => o.id`.

**Refs and generics.** `forwardRef` returns a non-generic component type, so wrapping a generic component used to lose T and required a cast or a module augmentation. React 19 passes `ref` as an ordinary prop, so you add `ref?: React.Ref<HTMLUListElement>` to the props and the component stays generic.

**When not to.** If a component only ever renders one data type, a generic adds noise. Generic components pay off in design systems and shared libraries: selects, comboboxes, tables, lists, virtualised grids, form fields bound to a schema.

**A `.d.ts` file contains only type declarations, no implementation: it describes the shape of JavaScript that exists somewhere else so the checker can type-check code that uses it. For an untyped dependency, first look for bundled types or a DefinitelyTyped package (`@types/x`); if neither exists, write a `declare module "x"` declaration covering the parts you use, and use the one-line `declare module "x";` shorthand only as a temporary escape hatch.**

**Where declarations come from.**

```text
1. The package ships them        package.json "types" or an exports "types" condition
2. DefinitelyTyped               npm i -D @types/x      (community maintained)
3. You write them                src/types/x.d.ts
4. Generated from your TS code   tsc --declaration (what libraries publish)
```

**Writing one for an untyped library.**

```ts
// src/types/tiny-charts.d.ts
declare module "tiny-charts" {
  export interface ChartOptions {
    width: number;
    height: number;
    series: Array<{ name: string; values: number[] }>;
    theme?: "light" | "dark";
  }
  export interface Chart {
    update(series: ChartOptions["series"]): void;
    destroy(): void;
  }
  export function createChart(el: HTMLElement, options: ChartOptions): Chart;
  export default createChart;
}
```

Cover only what you use, mark uncertain things conservatively (return `unknown` rather than guessing), and consider contributing the result to DefinitelyTyped. Make sure the file is included by your tsconfig `include`.

The shorthand `declare module "tiny-charts";` makes every import from it `any`. It unblocks a build in a minute; treat it as debt.

**Global vs module declaration files.** A `.d.ts` file with no top-level `import` or `export` is a global script: its declarations are visible everywhere. Adding a single top-level import turns it into a module, and its declarations become local, which is a classic "my global types disappeared" bug. To declare globals from a module file, use `declare global`:

```ts
// src/types/globals.d.ts
import type { Analytics } from "./analytics";

declare global {
  interface Window {
    analytics?: Analytics;
  }
}
export {};
```

**Module augmentation** adds to an existing module's types, relying on interface merging:

```ts
// Express: attach the authenticated user
import "express";
declare module "express-serve-static-core" {
  interface Request { user?: { id: string; role: "admin" | "member" } }
}
```

Augmentations only merge into interfaces (and namespaces); you cannot change a `type` alias or remove members.

**Assets.** Bundlers let you import non-JS files, so declare them:

```ts
declare module "*.svg" { const src: string; export default src; }
declare module "*.module.css" { const classes: Record<string, string>; export default classes; }
```

Frameworks usually ship these (`vite/client`, `next-env.d.ts`).

**Generated declarations.** Libraries emit `.d.ts` with `declaration: true` (plus `declarationMap` so "go to definition" lands in the source). `isolatedDeclarations` (5.5) requires explicit types on exports so other tools can generate declarations per file without running the checker.

**skipLibCheck** skips checking the contents of all `.d.ts` files, including your own hand-written ones, so a mistake in your declaration file may go unreported. Your code is still checked against the declarations, which is what matters most.

**It means a check at a boundary should not just say yes or no; it should turn loosely typed input into a more precisely typed value, so the proof of validity is carried by the type from then on. In TypeScript that usually means a schema (Zod, Valibot) that takes `unknown` and returns a typed object or an error, applied once where data enters, with the rest of the code relying on the types instead of re-checking.**

The phrase is the title of Alexis King's 2019 essay, written about Haskell but directly applicable.

**Validate vs parse.**

```ts
// Validator: knowledge is lost immediately
function isValidSignup(x: unknown): boolean { /* checks */ return true; }
if (isValidSignup(body)) createUser(body as Signup);   // a cast, and every caller must remember

// Parser: the type changes
const SignupSchema = z.object({
  email: z.email().transform((e) => e.toLowerCase()),
  password: z.string().min(12),
  plan: z.enum(["free", "pro"]).default("free"),
});
type Signup = z.infer<typeof SignupSchema>;

const result = SignupSchema.safeParse(body);
if (!result.success) return badRequest(z.flattenError(result.error).fieldErrors);
createUser(result.data);   // Signup: normalised email, plan defaulted
```

The parser also **normalises** (lowercases the email, fills defaults, converts strings to Dates), so downstream code receives one canonical shape.

**Make the output as precise as possible.** Parsing is the moment to encode what you learned:
- non-empty arrays (`[T, ...T[]]`) instead of `T[]` plus a length check later;
- discriminated unions instead of objects with many optional fields;
- branded types (`Email`, `UserId`) so unparsed strings cannot be passed where parsed ones are required;
- `Date` instead of ISO strings.

**Where the boundaries are (typical full-stack TS app).**

```text
inbound to server:  request bodies, query/route params, headers, cookies,
                    Server Action FormData, webhooks (after signature check), queue messages
inbound to client:  API responses, localStorage/IndexedDB, URL search params, postMessage
process start:      environment variables (parse once, crash early with a clear message)
AI features:        LLM JSON output, tool-call arguments
```

**Input vs output types.** With transforms and defaults, a schema has two types. `z.input<typeof S>` is what callers send (email any case, plan optional); `z.infer<typeof S>` (the output) is what you get after parsing. Forms and API clients that build requests want the input type; business logic wants the output.

**What it buys you.**
- No "shotgun parsing": checks scattered across the codebase, each slightly different, with some paths forgetting one.
- Errors surface at the edge with field paths, instead of a `TypeError` five calls later.
- Refactors are safe: change the schema and the compiler shows every consumer that is affected.

**Stored data.** Data in `localStorage` or a database outlives the code that wrote it. Version it (`{ v: 2, ... }`), parse with a discriminated union over versions, migrate old versions to the current shape in one function, and fall back to a default when parsing fails rather than crashing the app.

**Cost and limits.** Parsing is CPU work; for very large or hot payloads, parse once at ingestion and keep the typed result, or use a faster validator. And parsing proves shape, not truth: a well-formed `userId` still needs an authorisation check.

**TypeScript is structural, so `type UserId = string` and `type OrderId = string` are the same type and can be swapped by accident. A branded type intersects the base type with a phantom property that no real value has, such as `string & { readonly [brand]: "UserId" }`, which makes it incompatible with plain strings and other brands while staying a normal string at runtime. They are worth it for identifiers that are easy to mix up, units, and values that have passed validation or authorisation, created only by a small set of parsing functions.**

```ts
declare const brand: unique symbol;
export type Brand<T, B extends string> = T & { readonly [brand]: B };

export type UserId = Brand<string, "UserId">;
export type OrderId = Brand<string, "OrderId">;
export type Cents = Brand<number, "Cents">;

declare function refund(orderId: OrderId, amount: Cents): Promise<void>;

const userId = "usr_1" as UserId;
refund(userId, 500 as Cents);    // Error: UserId is not assignable to OrderId
refund("ord_1", 500);            // Error: plain string and number rejected
const asString: string = userId; // OK: still a string for every string API
```

**Why `unique symbol`.** A string key like `__brand` shows up in autocomplete, could collide with a real property, and two libraries using `__brand: "UserId"` would treat each other's ids as compatible. A non-exported `unique symbol` key cannot be written by anyone else and does not appear as a usable property.

**Creating branded values.** The whole value of a brand is that only trusted code can produce it. Put the only casts inside parsers:

```ts
const UserIdSchema = z.string().regex(/^usr_[a-z0-9]{10,}$/).brand<"UserId">();
type UserId = z.infer<typeof UserIdSchema>;

export function parseUserId(raw: string): UserId {
  return UserIdSchema.parse(raw);
}
```

Then route params, database rows and API responses are parsed into branded types at the boundary, and the core of the app never sees a raw string id. Valibot has an equivalent `brand` action, and the same pattern works without a library (a function with a check and one cast).

**Where brands pay off.**
- **Entity ids**: `UserId`, `OrderId`, `TenantId`. Multi-tenant code where a `tenantId` and a `userId` swap leaks data across tenants is the classic motivating bug.
- **Units**: `Cents` vs `Dollars`, `Milliseconds` vs `Seconds`, `Celsius` vs `Fahrenheit`. Unit mix-ups are famously expensive.
- **Validated or sanitised strings**: `Email`, `Slug`, `SanitizedHtml`, `AbsoluteUrl`, so a raw user string cannot reach a sink that expects a cleaned one.
- **Capability tokens**: a type that can only be produced by a function that performed an authorisation check.

**Costs and caveats.**
- Friction: every creation site needs a parser, and tests need small helpers to build fixtures.
- Brands vanish in serialisation: JSON from the network is just strings again, so parse on the way in.
- ORMs and query builders return plain types; wrap the data access layer to convert, or use tools that support branded columns.
- Arithmetic drops brands (`a + b` on two `Cents` is `number`), so unit types need helper functions for maths.
- Over-branding makes teams cast to get work done, which is worse than no brand. Start with ids and money.

**Alternatives.** Wrapper classes with a private field are truly nominal and can carry methods, at the cost of an allocation and no direct string APIs. For most web code, zero-cost brands created by schemas are the better default.
